# A short story for the Aside engine.
# Fourth register. Night Shift is a demo, Overtime is horror and performance, The Lamp Room is warm and about
# tending, The Discrepancy is quiet and wry. This one is elegiac and practical: an archivist in a city that is
# flooding, moving the collection up a floor every few years, deciding what gets carried and what gets left.
#
# The whole story is a sorting problem with a person attached to it. Nothing supernatural happens. Six scenes,
# three endings, one sitting.

title: The Water Line
author: Roxanne
start: basement

== basement ==
bg archive_basement
music sea_swell
The line is chalk, and it is higher than it was in March.

You drew it yourself, the first year, at knee height because knee height seemed like a lot. It is at your chest
now. The room is twenty metres of shelving and eleven of it is above the line.

The rule is simple and everyone knows it: what is above the line goes up, what is below it stays, and the water
is not sentimental.

* Start at the far end. Work backwards, the way you always do. -> far_end
* Start at the near end, where the reading room ledgers are. -> near_end
* Stand still a moment and look at the line. -> look

== look ==
It is only chalk. You know that. You drew it with a piece of chalk you took from the reading room and never
returned, and it has been redrawn by you every spring for nineteen years, and the shelf it was first drawn
against was moved up a floor in 2009.

You are the only person left who has seen every version of it.

* Start at the far end. -> far_end
* Start at the near end. -> near_end

== far_end ==
The far end is the municipal record: rate books, deeds, the planning minutes. Heavy, dull, irreplaceable in the
way that a foundation is irreplaceable.

The first three shelves are below the line. You take the rate books down and stack them on the trolley, and it
takes an hour, and your back says something about it that you do not repeat.

There is a box at the back that is not municipal. It has no call number. It has your handwriting on it.

* Open it. -> box
* Leave it. It is not on the list, and the list is how you have kept this place dry for nineteen years. -> leave_box

== near_end ==
The near end is the reading room ledgers: who came in, what they asked for, what they found. Nineteen years of
other people's curiosity in nineteen volumes.

They are all below the line.

Nobody has asked for a reading room ledger in eleven years. The last entry in the last volume is a woman asking
for a map of the harbour and writing, at the bottom, *thank you, this is the one my father used*.

* Take the ledgers. They are the only record of anyone having wanted anything. -> ledgers
* Leave them and go to the far end, where the records are. -> far_end

== box ==
The box is light. Inside is a card index, handwritten, one card per person who has ever used the reading room,
with a line under each name: what they were looking for.

Your own card is in it. The line under your name says: *the water line. How far it has come.*

You do not remember writing that. You do remember the first year, and the chalk, and thinking that knee height
seemed like a lot.

* Put the box on the trolley. -> box_up
* Put it back and take the rate books. -> far_end

== box_up ==
~ saved_personal true
The box goes on top of the rate books, which is the wrong order by every rule you have, and the right one by the
only rule that is going to matter tonight.

You take it up the stairs and set it on the new shelf and go back down for the next load.

-> flood

== leave_box ==
You put the box back where it was. It is not on the list. The list is how you have kept this place dry.

You take the rate books up instead, and the box stays below the line, and you tell yourself the water will not
come that far this year.

-> flood

== ledgers ==
~ saved_personal true
You take the ledgers. All nineteen, which is two trips, and on the second trip the water is already at the bottom
step and the chalk line is behind you.

-> flood

== flood ==
sfx static
The pump in the sub-basement fails at 4:10, which you know because the light changes when it does.
bg archive_stairs

The water comes up the room the way water does: without hurry, and without any interest in what it is covering.

* Go back down. There is time for one more shelf. -> one_more
* Stay at the top of the stairs and let it finish. -> watch

== one_more ==
The water is at your knees on the bottom flight and it is cold in a way that makes the decision for you about
bg archive_stairs
how far you are willing to go.

There is one shelf within reach. It is the planning minutes: dull, heavy, and the thing a city actually needs
when it wants to prove that a street was a street.

* Take the planning minutes. -> ending_official
* Take the box with your handwriting on it, if it is still there. -> ending_personal
* Take nothing. Go up. -> ending_nothing

== watch ==
You sit on the top step with your feet on the third one down, which stays dry, and you watch the line go under.
bg archive_stairs

It takes eleven minutes. You have never actually watched it happen before. It is the first year you have not
been carrying something.

* Go back down. There is time for one more shelf. -> one_more
* Let it be finished. -> ending_nothing

== ending_official ==
~ ending official
You carry the planning minutes up four flights, one volume at a time, and you are still carrying them when the
bg archive_upper
water reaches the ceiling of the room they came from.

The city will ask for those volumes in forty years and they will be here, dry, and the person who asks will not
know your name and will not need to.

You sleep in the reading room because the stairs are wet. In the morning the chalk line is under nine metres of
water and you draw a new one, higher, on a wall that is still dry.

-> ending

== ending_personal ==
~ ending personal
The box is still on its shelf when you get there, floating, lid down, and you go in up to your shoulders to get
bg archive_upper
it and the cards inside are ruined.

You take it up anyway. You spread the cards out on the reading room table and they dry badly and the ink runs,
and the one line you wanted is the one that goes first.

But you have the box, and the handwriting on it is still your handwriting, and that turns out to be the part you
were actually carrying.

-> ending

== ending_nothing ==
~ ending nothing
You go up. You do not take anything.
bg archive_upper

At the top of the stairs you sit down and let the building do what it has been going to do since the first
spring, and you think about the fact that a line is not a wall and never was, and that you have spent nineteen
years being very good at the part of the job that could be done with chalk.

In the morning you will be the only person who knows what was on the shelves, and that is a kind of archive too.

-> ending

== ending ==
The water stops at the second floor, which nobody expected, and stays there.
bg archive_upper

In the spring you go back down with a new piece of chalk. The room is a room again: eleven metres of silt, and
shelving standing in it like a burned forest, and light coming down through the reading room windows in a way it
never did when there were books in front of them.

You draw the line where the water stopped, because that is where it stopped, and you write the date beside it,
because that is what you do.

* Bring the box back down with you. -> ending_box [if saved_personal]
* Read the date out loud, to nobody. -> ending_date
* Admit that you are the archive. -> ending_self [if ending == "nothing"]
* Start bringing things back down. -> END

== ending_box ==
The box dried badly and the cards are stuck together in one block, and the line you wanted is the line that went
bg archive_upper
first.

You put it on the shelf it came off. It is above the new line now, which is the only thing you were ever
actually asked to do.

* Read the date out loud, to nobody. -> ending_date
* Start bringing things back down. -> END

== ending_date ==
"Fourth of April."
bg archive_upper

It sounds like nothing. It sounds like a date. Nineteen years of this and the whole job has been: notice where
the water is, and say so, and do it again next year.

* Start bringing things back down. -> END

== ending_self ==
You took nothing, and you are the only copy.
bg archive_upper

Every rate book, every planning minute, every card in that box: the water has them now, and what it does not
have is the nineteen years of knowing where they were. That is not a comfort and it is not a substitute. It is
just what is true.

You draw the line and you write the date and you go up, and next spring you come down again, and you are the
archive, and you had better last.

-> END
